Why the bytes are copied rather than rewritten
----------------------------------------------

zvcs is measured against stock git by byte-comparing the repositories the two
produce (`cargo run -p zvcs-parity`). `git init` copies this tree into every new
repository, so a `description` or a `pre-commit.sample` that merely *does the
same thing* is still a parity failure — only git's exact bytes pass. A
functionally equivalent rewrite would therefore not be an alternative to
copying; it would be a decision to fail those cases.

Reading the files off the machine at run time was the other option and is worse:
it would make a zvcs repository's contents a property of whichever git happens to
be installed beside it, and zvcs is built to keep working once stock git is
uninstalled.


Refreshing them
---------------

Copy `templates/description`, `templates/info/exclude` and
`templates/hooks/*.sample` out of the git tag being targeted, leaving the
`@PERL_PATH@` placeholder in place — `porcelain/init.rs` substitutes it at write
time, exactly as `templates/Makefile` does at install time, so what is stored
here stays byte-identical to git's sources rather than to one machine's install.

Two of the files are version-dependent: `hooks/commit-msg.sample` and
`hooks/fsmonitor-watchman.sample` differ between git 2.50.1 and 2.55.0. The
other fourteen are byte-identical across those releases. zvcs targets the newest
git, so v2.55.0's bytes are the ones stored here.
